      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, let's tackle the deflection first. For a 4x12 Douglas fir beam spanning 14 feet with a total load of 45 psf, we can use the following formula to calculate the maximum deflection:\n\nDeflection (Δ) = (5 * w * L^4) / (384 * E * I)\n\nWhere:\n- w = load per unit length (45 psf)\n- L = span length (14 ft)\n- E = modulus of elasticity (approximately 1,600,000 psi for Douglas fir)\n- I = moment of inertia (for a 4x12 beam, I ≈ 1,200 in^4)\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\nΔ = (5 * 45 * 14^4) / (384 * 1,600,000 * 1,200)\nΔ ≈ 0.22 inches\n\nNow, let's talk about the L/360 deflection limit. For a 14-foot span, the maximum allowable deflection is:\n\nL/360 = 14 / 360 ≈ 0.0389 inches\n\nSince 0.22 inches is greater than 0.0389 inches, your beam will not meet the L/360",
